T24.01|Banks create deposit liabilities when granting qualifying loans, while acquiring a loan asset. Lending is constrained by credit demand, underwriting, capital, liquidity, funding and regulation. The process is not an unlimited ability to create economically valuable money without risk.~~When the borrower spends to another bank's customer, the lending bank may need to settle with reserves or other available funding. Loan creation and subsequent payment liquidity are linked but distinct processes.|Credit creation: Loan asset and deposit liability arise together.;;Intermediation: Funding and risk are transformed through the bank.;;Settlement: Payments require eligible resources between institutions.|A bank grants a ₹10 lakh loan and credits the borrower's deposit. Ignore fees and regulatory calculations.~~Loan assets rise ₹10 and deposits rise ₹10.;;The bank's equity does not rise by ₹10 at inception.;;If the borrower transfers the deposit elsewhere, the bank must meet the settlement outflow.~~Creating a loan also creates repayment exposure and potential funding needs.~~Interest income becomes economic benefit only after funding costs, credit losses and operating expenses.|Track assets and liabilities together.;;Separate lending from payment settlement.;;Assess capital, liquidity and credit constraints.
T24.02|A bank's balance sheet differs from an industrial firm's: loans and securities are major assets, while deposits and wholesale borrowing fund them. Equity absorbs residual losses. Its earnings depend on interest spread, fees, operating costs and credit losses.~~Cash, capital and accounting profit are distinct. A capital ratio concerns loss absorption relative to risk exposures, while liquidity concerns paying cash obligations. High reported earnings do not establish good loan quality.|Earning assets: Loans and investments generating revenue.;;Funding liabilities: Deposits and borrowed funds.;;Capital: Eligible loss-absorbing claims under stated definitions.|Loans and other assets total ₹100 lakh, funded by deposits ₹80, borrowing ₹10 and equity ₹10. A ₹3 loan loss is recognised, with no other changes.~~Assets fall to ₹97.;;Liabilities remain ₹90.;;Equity falls to ₹7.~~A small loss relative to assets can be large relative to equity.~~Regulatory capital calculations may differ from this simplified accounting-equity illustration.|Read asset quality and funding composition.;;Reconcile earnings and provisions.;;Assess leverage and capital definitions.
T24.03|Deposit and wholesale funding differ in behaviour, maturity, concentration and cost. Retail transaction balances, term deposits, secured borrowing and market debt can respond differently to stress. Contractual maturity alone does not describe actual stability.~~A funding strategy should consider withdrawal sensitivity, rollover needs, collateral and currency. A low-cost concentrated deposit base can leave a bank vulnerable to a few large withdrawals.|Transaction deposits: Payment-oriented balances under account terms.;;Term deposits: Funding with stated tenor and withdrawal conditions.;;Wholesale funding: Larger institutional or market borrowing.|A bank has ₹100 lakh deposits, of which one customer owns ₹40. That customer withdraws ₹30.~~The withdrawal removes 30% of total deposit funding.;;Liquidity must meet the payment even if assets remain valuable.;;Compare this exposure with a diversified base of many smaller depositors.~~Concentration can dominate a seemingly comfortable aggregate funding total.~~Deposit insurance, withdrawal rights and behavioural stability depend on the jurisdiction and product.|Map concentration and maturity.;;Stress withdrawals and rollover.;;Include collateral and currency constraints.
T24.04|Net interest income is interest earned less interest expense. Net interest margin divides net interest income by an appropriately defined average earning-asset base. A rising asset yield does not guarantee better margin if funding reprices faster.~~Analyse asset mix, deposit costs, hedges and credit quality. Accrued interest that is not collectible can overstate sustainable economics if recognition and impairment do not reflect deterioration.|Asset yield: Interest income/average earning assets.;;Funding cost: Interest expense/defined average funding base.;;Net interest margin: Net interest income/average earning assets.|Average earning assets are ₹100 lakh, interest income ₹8 and interest expense ₹4.~~Net interest income = ₹8 − ₹4 = ₹4.;;NIM = ₹4/₹100 = 4%.;;If funding expense rises to ₹5 with asset income unchanged, NIM falls to 3%.~~Funding repricing reduces margin without a change in loan balances.~~Use average earning assets rather than total closing assets unless the metric explicitly specifies otherwise.|State denominator definitions.;;Trace repricing and mix.;;Assess whether accrued income is supported by collections.
T24.05|Fee income comes from services such as payments, distribution, custody or advisory activity. Operating efficiency compares costs with an appropriate income base, often through a cost-to-income ratio. Business mix and revenue volatility affect interpretation.~~Low costs achieved through inadequate controls or service can create later losses. Gross fees also may not equal retained fees after commissions, refunds and associated operating expenses.|Recurring service fees: Ongoing payment or account activity.;;Transaction fees: Revenue linked to discrete deals or volumes.;;Cost-to-income ratio: Operating expense/defined operating income.|Assume net interest income ₹40 lakh, retained fees ₹20 and operating expense ₹30.~~Operating income before credit costs is ₹60.;;Cost-to-income ratio = ₹30/₹60 = 50%.;;If fees fall to ₹10 while costs stay ₹30, the ratio rises to 60%.~~A worse ratio can reflect lower income rather than higher spending.~~Separate credit losses and unusual items according to the metric's stated definition.|Reconcile gross and retained fees.;;Use consistent cost and income scope.;;Assess resilience and control quality.
T24.06|Asset-liability management coordinates interest-rate, liquidity and currency exposures across a bank's balance sheet. Repricing gaps affect near-term income, while duration and economic-value measures assess sensitivity of asset and liability values. A position can look safe on one dimension and exposed on another.~~Behavioural assumptions matter for deposits, prepayments and drawdowns. Hedging market-value sensitivity may alter short-term income or collateral needs.|Repricing gap: Timing of rate resets.;;Liquidity gap: Timing of cash obligations and resources.;;Economic-value sensitivity: Value changes under rate scenarios.|Floating-rate loans of ₹80 lakh reset immediately, while ₹60 floating funding resets immediately. Assume a 1-point rate rise and no other effects.~~Rate-sensitive gap = ₹20.;;Simplified annual NII effect = ₹20 × 1% = +₹0.20.;;Separately assess fixed-rate assets and liabilities for value sensitivity.~~A positive income gap does not establish low total balance-sheet risk.~~Deposit behaviour, basis differences and nonlinear options can change the result.|Map reset and maturity dates separately.;;Test behavioural assumptions.;;Assess both income and economic value under stress.
T24.07|Capital adequacy assesses eligible loss-absorbing capital against risk-weighted exposures under the relevant framework. Risk-weighted assets differ from total accounting assets because risk categories, models and other exposure rules matter. Capital tiers also differ in their eligibility and loss-absorption features.~~A capital ratio is not a cash reserve ratio. Regulatory requirements, buffers and national implementation vary; illustrative percentages should not be presented as universally applicable minima.|CET1: Highest-quality common-equity capital under framework definitions.;;Additional tier capital: Other eligible instruments with specified features.;;Risk-weighted assets: Regulatory risk-adjusted exposure base.|Assume eligible capital ₹12 lakh and risk-weighted assets ₹100. A loss reduces capital by ₹3 with RWA unchanged.~~Initial capital ratio = 12/100 = 12%.;;After the loss, capital is ₹9.;;The ratio becomes 9%, before other regulatory adjustments.~~Losses can materially reduce regulatory headroom.~~This is arithmetic, not a claim that either ratio complies with a particular bank's requirements.|Identify capital tier and adjustments.;;Read applicable requirements and buffers.;;Stress both losses and changing RWA.
T24.08|Bank liquidity risk concerns meeting cash obligations without unacceptable loss. Funding risk includes inability to replace maturing or withdrawn funding on workable terms. Regulatory liquidity measures and internal stress tests examine related but different horizons and assumptions.~~LCR focuses on a specified short stress horizon using eligible liquid assets and modelled net outflows; NSFR addresses structural funding stability. Actual compliance requires current local implementation, not a classroom balance calculation.|Short-term liquidity: Payment capacity under immediate stress.;;Structural funding: Stability relative to longer-term assets.;;Market liquidity: Ability to sell or finance assets without major loss.|Assume accessible cash and saleable securities after haircuts total ₹20 lakh, while a scenario requires ₹25 cash.~~Stress liquidity resources are ₹20.;;The immediate gap is ₹5.;;Identify dependable additional funding or balance-sheet actions before the stress occurs.~~A solvent bank can still have a liquidity shortfall.~~This example is not an LCR calculation because regulatory eligibility and net-outflow rules are not specified.|Count resources after restrictions and haircuts.;;Stress correlated outflows and collateral demands.;;Assess rollover and currency-specific needs.
T24.09|Loan underwriting assesses whether a borrower can repay from realistic primary cash sources. Collateral is a secondary recovery source, not a replacement for viable cash flow. Origination includes verification, pricing, structure, documentation and monitoring requirements.~~Evaluate purpose, borrower incentives, leverage, repayment timing and downside. Historical profitability may not represent future service capacity if the loan funds a risky expansion or faces refinancing concentration.|Cash-flow lending: Repayment depends mainly on operating generation.;;Asset-backed lending: Eligible collateral and controls are central.;;Consumer underwriting: Income, affordability and borrower behaviour matter.|A borrower generates ₹30 lakh cash available for debt service and proposes annual service ₹25.~~Base coverage = 30/25 = 1.2.;;At a 25% cash decline, available cash is ₹22.5.;;Stress coverage = 22.5/25 = 0.9.~~A modest base cushion becomes a stress shortfall.~~Assess other reserves, payment flexibility and recoveries rather than relying on one coverage ratio.|Verify primary repayment sources.;;Stress assumptions and timing.;;Structure covenants, security and monitoring to the actual risk.
T24.10|Retail, SME and corporate loans require different information and monitoring. Retail portfolios often use standardised scoring and affordability checks; SME loans need attention to owner dependence and cash records; corporate loans require business, group and contractual analysis.~~The categories are not risk rankings. A well-secured small borrower can be safer than a concentrated large corporate, while standardised models can miss individual circumstances or data problems.|Retail: Household or individual exposure.;;SME: Smaller businesses with distinct operational and information risks.;;Corporate: Larger entity or group credit with negotiated structures.|A household owes monthly payments ₹2 against dependable income ₹5; an SME has variable cash flow; a corporation has concentrated refinancing.~~Assess affordability for the household.;;Evaluate seasonal receipts and owner withdrawals for the SME.;;Map maturities and group obligations for the corporation.~~The underwriting emphasis changes with the repayment mechanism.~~All three need evidence, stress analysis and fair treatment under applicable rules.|Identify borrower and purpose.;;Use relevant income and cash evidence.;;Monitor risks specific to the business or household.
T24.11|Credit ratings express assessed credit quality under an agency or internal scale. Credit scores quantify selected predictive information, often for standardised borrower decisions. Neither is a guarantee of repayment or a substitute for understanding the exposure.~~Ratings may concern an issuer or a particular instrument, which can differ because of priority and collateral. Scores depend on data, model calibration and population; an old score may not reflect new deterioration.|Issuer rating: Broad obligor credit assessment.;;Issue rating: Instrument-specific assessment.;;Credit score: Model-based numerical indicator under a defined methodology.|Two loans to the same borrower have identical default exposure, but one has first-ranking collateral and the other is unsecured.~~Borrower default likelihood may be shared.;;Expected recoveries can differ because legal priority differs.;;Instrument assessments can therefore differ from the issuer assessment.~~Default probability and loss severity are separate dimensions.~~Security value, enforceability and competing claims must be verified rather than assumed.|Read rating scope and scale.;;Check score data and recency.;;Assess recovery structure independently.
T24.12|Probability of default estimates the chance of a specified default event over a stated horizon. Loss given default estimates the share of exposure lost conditional on default after relevant recoveries and costs. Exposure at default estimates the amount outstanding at that event.~~A simplified expected-loss formula is PD × LGD × EAD. Full models may require multiple dates, discounting, scenarios and dependence; multiplying three headline averages can miss these effects.|PD: Horizon-specific default likelihood.;;LGD: Conditional loss share.;;EAD: Amount exposed when default occurs.|Assume one-year PD 2%, LGD 40% and EAD ₹100 lakh, with no timing adjustment.~~Conditional loss = ₹100 × 40% = ₹40.;;Expected loss = 2% × ₹40 = ₹0.8.;;At PD 5% with other assumptions fixed, expected loss rises to ₹2.~~Low default frequency can still create material expected loss on large exposure.~~The ₹0.8 is a model average, not a prediction that every loan loses exactly that amount.|State default definition and horizon.;;Estimate recoveries and drawdowns.;;Distinguish expected loss from tail loss and capital.
T24.13|Collateral provides an asset claim; a guarantee provides another party's payment promise. Security structure determines priority, perfection and enforcement. Their value depends on legal effectiveness, recovery timing, market value and correlation with borrower distress.~~A guarantor exposed to the same shock may fail when support is needed. Collateral can lose value during default, and book value may exceed recoverable proceeds after costs.|Collateral: Claim on specified assets.;;Guarantee: Payment obligation of another party.;;Seniority and security: Priority and enforceability among competing claims.|A ₹100 lakh loan has collateral expected to sell for ₹80, with recovery costs ₹10. Assume no competing claim and immediate sale.~~Net recovery = ₹80 − ₹10 = ₹70.;;Loss = ₹100 − ₹70 = ₹30.;;Simplified LGD = 30%.~~A secured loan can still suffer substantial loss.~~Delay, litigation, prior claims and correlated asset-price declines can reduce present-value recovery further.|Verify title and perfection.;;Stress recovery value and timing.;;Assess guarantor strength and competing priorities.
T24.14|Covenants define contractual promises, restrictions and financial tests. Debt-service capacity concerns cash available for required interest and principal. Covenant EBITDA or cash definitions can differ from financial-statement figures and from analyst estimates.~~A breach can change lender rights or liquidity conditions even when payments are currently made. Assess headroom under realistic stresses and verify cure, waiver and acceleration provisions.|Maintenance covenant: Periodic compliance test.;;Incurrence covenant: Test triggered by specified actions.;;Payment capacity: Cash available relative to debt service.|Assume cash available for debt service ₹30 lakh and annual service ₹20, with a covenant minimum of 1.3 under the same simplified definition.~~DSCR = 30/20 = 1.5.;;A 20% cash decline gives ₹24 and DSCR 1.2.;;The borrower could pay ₹20 yet fail the stated 1.3 covenant.~~Payment ability and covenant compliance are distinct.~~The minimum is hypothetical; actual agreements determine calculation and remedies.|Read exact covenant definitions.;;Calculate headroom and downside.;;Map breach consequences and maturities.
T24.15|Non-performing classification identifies credit deterioration under applicable supervisory or accounting criteria. Provisioning estimates recognised loss allowances; a write-off removes amounts under its governing conditions. Classification, accounting loss and cash recovery are connected but different concepts.~~Do not apply one country's overdue-day rule universally. A loan may warrant concern before a formal threshold, and a provision does not mean cash has been recovered or paid out.|Non-performing status: Regulatory or defined credit classification.;;Loss provision: Recognised expected or required loss estimate.;;Write-off and recovery: Removal and later collection where relevant.|Gross loans are ₹100 lakh and loss allowance ₹8. Assume ₹3 is written off against the allowance.~~Before write-off, net carrying amount is ₹92.;;After this isolated write-off, gross loans ₹97 and allowance ₹5 still net to ₹92.;;No cash collection occurs merely from the write-off.~~A lower gross balance need not indicate improved collection.~~Additional allowance reassessment or later recoveries would create separate movements.|Read the governing classification criteria.;;Reconcile gross loans and allowances.;;Track actual recoveries and restructuring separately.
T24.16|Expected credit losses estimate probability-weighted discounted cash shortfalls under the relevant accounting model. They incorporate forward-looking information rather than waiting only for a realised default. Under IFRS 9's general approach, significant credit deterioration can change the measurement horizon from 12-month to lifetime losses; simplified approaches exist for specified exposures.~~Twelve-month ECL refers to lifetime shortfalls arising from defaults possible in the next twelve months, not simply cash missed within twelve months. Accounting ECL and regulatory expected-loss calculations need not be identical.|12-month ECL: Losses associated with defaults possible over the next twelve months under the model.;;Lifetime ECL: Losses from possible defaults over the expected life.;;Scenario weighting: Combine outcomes using defensible forward-looking probabilities.|For a simplified one-period illustration, base expected loss is ₹1 lakh with weight 70% and stressed expected loss ₹4 with weight 30%.~~Weighted ECL = 0.7 × ₹1 + 0.3 × ₹4 = ₹1.9.;;If stress weight rises to 50%, ECL becomes ₹2.5.;;Reassess the horizon and assumptions separately if credit risk deteriorates.~~Changed forward-looking weights affect the allowance before a default occurs.~~A full application also requires timing, discounting, recoveries and framework-specific rules.|Identify the applicable ECL approach.;;Assess deterioration and forward-looking evidence.;;Document horizon, scenarios and validation.
T24.17|Restructuring modifies loan terms to address repayment difficulty or a viable refinancing need. Recovery compares continuation, enforcement, sale and compromise options based on timing, cost and expected proceeds. A longer repayment schedule can relieve liquidity without restoring economic value.~~Assess borrower viability and incentives rather than repeatedly extending an unpayable obligation. Accounting, prudential classification and legal outcomes require the applicable framework and jurisdiction.|Term modification: Change in dates, rates or obligations.;;Workout: Negotiated recovery strategy.;;Enforcement or sale: Realise claims through legal or market routes.|A lender is offered ₹70 lakh immediately or ₹85 in two years. Use a hypothetical 15% rate for the delayed risky receipt.~~Delayed PV = 85/1.15² ≈ ₹64.27.;;Compare with ₹70 immediate recovery.;;Review execution probability, costs and alternatives before choosing.~~A larger nominal promise can be worth less than a smaller immediate recovery.~~The discount rate and recovery probabilities must fit the case; legal priority may constrain available choices.|Assess viable cash generation.;;Compare dated net recoveries.;;Track classification, incentives and execution risk.
T24.18|NBFCs, microfinance institutions and housing-finance providers differ in mandates, funding access, borrower segments and regulation. Many do not have the same deposit franchise or central-bank access as banks. Funding maturity and concentration can therefore dominate liquidity risk.~~Analyse the actual institution rather than assuming the category implies one risk profile. Housing loans may have collateral but long duration; microfinance portfolios can involve correlated local income and collection risks.|NBFC model: Non-bank credit intermediation under applicable rules.;;Microfinance: Small-ticket lending with segment-specific collection risks.;;Housing finance: Property-linked long-term lending.|A lender holds five-year loans ₹100 lakh funded by one-year borrowing ₹80 and equity ₹20.~~Map the ₹80 funding maturity before loan collections complete.;;Assess rollover access and liquidity reserves.;;Stress a market closure even if loan defaults remain low.~~Good asset quality does not eliminate maturity-mismatch risk.~~Actual deposit permissions and funding rules require jurisdiction-specific verification.|Identify permitted activities and funding.;;Map maturity and borrower concentration.;;Stress both credit and rollover risk.
T24.19|Securitisation packages loan cash flows into claims with specified priorities; loan sales transfer exposures under their agreements. Senior and junior tranches allocate losses differently, while servicing, guarantees and retained interests may leave the originator exposed.~~Legal sale, accounting derecognition and economic risk transfer are separate assessments. A transaction can raise liquidity while retaining significant loss exposure or creating contingent obligations.|Whole-loan sale: Transfer of individual or pooled loans.;;Tranched securitisation: Claims with differing payment and loss priority.;;Retained risk: Servicing, guarantees or junior holdings remain.|A ₹100 lakh pool supports ₹80 senior and ₹20 junior claims. Assume losses first reduce the junior tranche.~~A ₹10 pool loss leaves junior value ₹10 and senior principal unaffected in this simple waterfall.;;A ₹25 loss exhausts junior ₹20.;;The remaining ₹5 loss reaches senior claims.~~Tranching reallocates losses rather than removing the pool's credit risk.~~Real structures add timing, fees, triggers and legal complexities that can alter cash distributions.|Read the waterfall and triggers.;;Identify retained and contingent exposure.;;Assess legal transfer and accounting separately.
